# shir_nodes/substitute.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# POSIX `s/pattern/replacement/flags` applied to the LINES of a text
# (lowered from `sed s///`). REPLACES the old `RegSub`, which carried
# `global: bool` + `line_mode: bool` — two booleans that encoded four
# meanings over two different domains (a string vs a list of lines), set
# inconsistently at two call sites. That is the same class of ambiguity
# that shipped `wc -l` as `length + 1`; one node, one domain, one meaning.
#
# meaning: substitute pattern repl occ s == the lines of `s` (split at
#   '\n', the empty string after a FINAL newline dropped), each line with
#   the LEFTMOST (or, with Global, EVERY non-overlapping) occurrence of the
#   POSIX BRE-ish pattern replaced by `repl`, re-joined with '\n' and given
#   a final newline iff `s` had one. `&` in `repl` is the matched text and
#   `\N` a back-reference (what sed does); an EMPTY match advances by one
#   byte, as POSIX specifies, so `s/x*/-/g` terminates.
#
# NOT covered (refuse, never guess):
#   * `-n` + a `p` flag (that is a filter, not a value — a print flag makes
#     the command's OUTPUT differ from its input line set);
#   * addresses (`2,4s///`), `d`, `y///`, multiple `-e` scripts (each is a
#     different program shape and each needs its own node or a composition);
#   * ERE/extended syntax selection beyond the flags recorded here.
#
# Backends: Perl s///, JS replace, Go regexp.ReplaceAllString, Rust regex,
#   C regcomp/regexec
# Fallback: sh2.substitute(text, pattern, repl, flags)
#
# Provenance: sh2perl/docs/fm/TextOps/Substitute.thy

node Substitute
tag "Substitute"
kind expr
field text: expr
field pattern: string
field replacement: string
# global: every non-overlapping match (sed's `g` flag), not just the first.
field global: bool
# flags the lowering accepted but that do not change the VALUE; anything
# value-changing (`p`, `w file`, an address) is refused before this node
# is built rather than carried here.
field ignore_case: bool
spec: TextOps#substitute_sound
